1
00:00:01,400 --> 00:00:03,702
Good morning, listeners.

2
00:00:03,652 --> 00:00:07,255
Today is November 11, 2026.

3
00:00:07,255 --> 00:00:14,314
Overnight, the Dow, S and P, and Nasdaq fell as Treasury yields surged on Fed hike bets.

4
00:00:14,314 --> 00:00:23,211
The S and P 500 lost 29 points, down 0.38 percent, forming a tight doji between short-term averages.

5
00:00:23,211 --> 00:00:32,697
The Nasdaq Composite shed 187 points, or 0.74 percent, while holding above its 250-day moving average.

6
00:00:32,697 --> 00:00:40,552
The Philadelphia Semiconductor Index slipped 0.35 percent after failing to reclaim its 250-day line.

7
00:00:40,552 --> 00:00:44,425
Hot October inflation triggered the cross-asset reversal.

8
00:00:44,425 --> 00:00:51,692
Headline CPI rose 0.4 percent month over month, topping the 0.3 percent forecast.

9
00:00:51,692 --> 00:00:58,983
Core CPI rose 0.3 percent for a third straight month, beating the 0.2 percent estimate.

10
00:00:58,983 --> 00:01:04,608
Last October's government shutdown prevented the release of year-over-year CPI figures.

11
00:01:04,608 --> 00:01:12,782
Energy prices jumped 0.7 percent, gasoline gained 1 percent, and shelter rose 0.3 percent.

12
00:01:12,782 --> 00:01:21,655
Core goods and non-housing services also accelerated, confirming sticky endogenous demand backed by a 4.1 percent unemployment rate.

13
00:01:21,655 --> 00:01:26,410
Federal Reserve officials responded with their most hawkish rhetoric of the cycle.

14
00:01:26,410 --> 00:01:33,125
Governor Waller backed a 25-basis-point December hike and warned a 50-basis-point move is possible.

15
00:01:33,125 --> 00:01:39,069
Waller stressed the central bank must claw back at least three prior precautionary rate cuts.

16
00:01:39,069 --> 00:01:47,537
Dallas Fed President Logan projected two more 25-basis-point hikes, while Chicago's Goolsbee warned against economic overheating.

17
00:01:47,537 --> 00:01:55,270
Governor Bowman estimated energy and AI demand will push October core PCE inflation to 3.3 percent.

18
00:01:55,270 --> 00:02:03,101
Still, Bowman cautioned that tighter financial conditions matter, citing over 800 billion dollars in AI debt risk.

19
00:02:03,101 --> 00:02:08,616
Swap markets now price a 100 percent probability of a December 9 rate hike.

20
00:02:08,616 --> 00:02:16,483
Traders assign a 90.5 percent chance to 25 basis points and 9.5 percent to 50 basis points.

21
00:02:16,483 --> 00:02:23,787
Two-year Treasury yields logged their biggest jump since April 2025, while ten-year yields neared 5 percent.

22
00:02:23,787 --> 00:02:28,297
The US dollar rose and spot gold tumbled as liquidity tightened.

23
00:02:28,297 --> 00:02:35,123
Meanwhile, crude oil pulled back after Qatar secured a two-week diplomatic window for US-Iran talks.

24
00:02:35,123 --> 00:02:42,157
Domestic policy uncertainty also climbed after President Trump named hardliner Stephen Miller as Chief of Staff.

25
00:02:42,157 --> 00:02:46,765
Under the surface, institutional positioning reveals a dangerous split.

26
00:02:46,765 --> 00:02:51,949
Single-stock hedge funds sold tech at the fastest weekly pace in four months.

27
00:02:51,949 --> 00:03:00,111
Yet macro trend-following funds remain heavily long index futures, creating 45 billion dollars in potential forced selling.

28
00:03:00,111 --> 00:03:06,741
The S and P 500 sits inside a negative gamma zone between 7000 and 7550.

29
00:03:06,741 --> 00:03:12,954
A technical breakdown could force options dealers and quant models into a self-reinforcing selloff.

30
00:03:12,954 --> 00:03:19,498
Conversely, Morgan Stanley sees the pullback nearing a bottom between 7100 and 7300.

31
00:03:19,498 --> 00:03:25,466
The VIX hovers near 20, while its put-to-call ratio hit a two-year high of 1.5.

32
00:03:25,466 --> 00:03:31,250
This macro rate shock directly hits the artificial intelligence capital expenditure cycle.

33
00:03:31,250 --> 00:03:38,273
Rate hikes cannot boost oil supply or alter tariffs, leaving rate-sensitive AI investment bearing the brunt.

34
00:03:38,273 --> 00:03:45,184
Outside of Microsoft, free cash flow across major cloud hyperscalers turned negative this earnings season.

35
00:03:45,184 --> 00:03:52,782
SoftBank posted its first quarterly loss in two years after a 3.7-billion-dollar US data center writedown.

36
00:03:52,782 --> 00:04:00,000
That loss pushed SoftBank's five-year credit default swap spreads to their widest level since October 2022.

37
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:06,925
Neocloud provider CoreWeave reported a narrower-than-expected loss of 1 dollar and 27 cents per share.

38
00:04:06,925 --> 00:04:16,765
CoreWeave's revenue surged 168 percent to 3.66 billion dollars, supported by a 132.5-billion-dollar backlog.

39
00:04:16,765 --> 00:04:25,417
However, 14.5 billion dollars in quarterly capital spending erased its 9 percent after-hours pop on borrowing cost fears.

40
00:04:25,544 --> 00:04:31,302
Despite financing friction, physical semiconductor hardware demand remains robust.

41
00:04:31,252 --> 00:04:38,801
Morgan Stanley trimmed data center capacity forecasts by just 5 percent due to project shifts into 2029.

42
00:04:38,801 --> 00:04:47,502
Analysts still project AI capital spending at 1.6 trillion dollars in 2027 and 1.8 trillion in 2028.

43
00:04:47,502 --> 00:04:55,309
Taiwan Semiconductor posted a 60 percent year-over-year October revenue surge, defying seasonal patterns to close higher.

44
00:04:55,309 --> 00:05:02,282
Bank of America and TrendForce expect the global memory supercycle to run through 2028 without a downturn.

45
00:05:02,282 --> 00:05:10,554
Global memory revenue is projected to rise from 970 billion dollars this year to 1.58 trillion by 2028.

46
00:05:10,554 --> 00:05:24,377
The ultimate liquidity test arrives next week with Anthropic's Nasdaq IPO under ticker A N T. Anthropic plans to price 825 million shares between 70 and 78 dollars next Wednesday for a Thursday debut.

47
00:05:24,377 --> 00:05:32,380
The offering will raise up to 64.4 billion dollars publicly alongside 26 billion in big-tech private placements.

48
00:05:32,380 --> 00:05:40,627
That 90.4-billion-dollar total haul values Anthropic between 1 trillion and 1.4 trillion dollars fully diluted.

49
00:05:40,627 --> 00:05:46,240
Underwriters cut the valuation from 2 trillion dollars to leave room for a first-day pop.

50
00:05:46,240 --> 00:05:54,059
While nine-month revenue topped 30 billion dollars, Anthropic carries 900 billion dollars in compute lease obligations.

51
00:05:54,059 --> 00:06:02,698
Anthropic also launched Claude Sheets and Whiteboards, directly challenging Microsoft 365's core enterprise franchise.

52
00:06:02,698 --> 00:06:09,279
In individual equities, three high-conviction setups offer clear tactical entry and risk levels today.

53
00:06:09,279 --> 00:06:16,534
First is Nvidia, ticker N V D A, which broke below 170 dollars amid a governance storm.

54
00:06:16,534 --> 00:06:24,034
Shareholders filed a class-action lawsuit over CEO Jensen Huang's disclosures, prompting an independent board probe.

55
00:06:24,034 --> 00:06:29,328
Nvidia and Lam Research were also removed from an institutional conviction list.

56
00:06:29,328 --> 00:06:37,257
Because Nvidia uses a single-class share structure and Huang owns just 3.4 percent, bears fear leadership risk.

57
00:06:37,257 --> 00:06:44,132
Huang manages 60 direct reports without a clear successor, and an exit could erase 30 percent in value.

58
00:06:44,132 --> 00:06:51,056
However, share turnover was only 1.7 percent, showing core institutional holders are not selling.

59
00:06:51,056 --> 00:06:57,037
Prediction markets price Huang's departure odds this year between just 14 and 17 percent.

60
00:06:57,037 --> 00:07:05,995
Nvidia's 300-billion-dollar ecosystem financing moat and 70 percent projected growth remain intact ahead of November 17 earnings.

61
00:07:05,995 --> 00:07:11,755
Tactical buyers should watch 164 dollars as the core left-side support entry.

62
00:07:11,755 --> 00:07:18,728
Holding 164 dollars targets a gap-fill rebound to 180 to 185 dollars.

63
00:07:18,728 --> 00:07:27,453
A decisive break below 164 dollars triggers a stop-loss, opening downside to 140 to 150 dollars.

64
00:07:27,453 --> 00:07:35,272
Second is Alphabet, ticker G O O G L, which unveiled its eighth-generation TPU AI system.

65
00:07:35,272 --> 00:07:42,184
The new architecture doubles compute capacity within the same footprint, strengthening Google's custom chip edge.

66
00:07:42,184 --> 00:07:49,855
Shares have consolidated since late October's high-volume earnings drop ahead of next week's Berkshire Hathaway 13F filing.

67
00:07:49,855 --> 00:07:54,059
The critical bull-bear pivot sits at 270 dollars.

68
00:07:54,059 --> 00:08:00,627
Holding above 270 dollars confirms a golden pit accumulation base for a tactical buy entry.

69
00:08:00,627 --> 00:08:07,515
A breakdown below 270 dollars traps post-earnings buyers and triggers an immediate stop-loss exit.

70
00:08:07,515 --> 00:08:14,402
Third is GE Vernova, ticker G E V, which fell 10 percent intraday before paring losses.

71
00:08:14,402 --> 00:08:21,363
Sellers reacted after SoftBank adjusted power equipment plans for OpenAI's Ohio Stargate data center.

72
00:08:21,363 --> 00:08:28,961
JPMorgan reiterated its overweight buy rating and 1035-dollar target, calling the dip an overreaction.

73
00:08:28,961 --> 00:08:34,206
GE Vernova's gas turbine capacity is booked solid through 2032.

74
00:08:34,206 --> 00:08:41,105
Traditional utilities represent 80 percent of contracted volume, while data centers account for just 20 percent.

75
00:08:41,105 --> 00:08:47,772
That backlog visibility makes the pullback an actionable buy entry targeting 1035 dollars.

76
00:08:47,772 --> 00:08:51,779
That concludes today's XiaoFang Finance Morning Brief.

77
00:08:51,779 --> 00:08:55,946
Thank you for listening, trade safely, and see you tomorrow.
